{{also|beissen|béissen}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|de|beissen}} {{qualifier|Switzerland|Liechtenstein}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|de|bʰeyd}}
From {{inh|de|goh|bīzan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*bītaną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-||to split}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|bieten}}, {{cog|nl|bijten}}, {{cog|en|bite}}, {{cog|da|bide}}, {{cog|sv|bita}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbaɪ̯sn̩/|/ˈbaɪ̯sən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-beißen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=1|beißt|biss|gebissen|past subjunctive=bisse}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[bite]]
# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[sting]]; to [[burn]]; to be [[sharp]]; to be [[spicy]]
# {{lb|de|reflexive|slightly|informal|of colors and clothes}} to [[clash]]; to [[jar]]

====Usage notes====
* In English, bloodsuckers are said to “bite”. In German, {{m|de||beißen}} is generally used with crawling bloodsuckers only, while {{m|de|stechen|t=to sting}} is used with flying bloodsuckers. Thus, you speak of a {{m|de|Flohbiss|t=flea bite}}, but a {{m|de|Mückenstich|t=mosquite bite}}.

